Given that $|z_1| = \left|\frac{1}{2}\right|$ and $|z_2| = \left|\frac{3}{8}\right|$, we have

\begin{align*}
|z_1 + z_2| &= \sqrt{(x_1 + x_2)^2 + (y_1 + y_2)^2}\\
&\leq \sqrt{x_1^2 + y_1^2} + \sqrt{x_2^2 + y_2^2}\\
&= \sqrt{|z_1|^2} + \sqrt{|z_2|^2}\\
&= \left|\frac{1}{2}\right| + \left|\frac{3}{8}\right|
\end{align*}

We want to minimize $|z_1+z_2|,$ so we work towards equality in the constraint above. Specifically, we can minimize the square of the left-hand side of the above equation, since $|x|^2 + |y|^2 = (|x|+|y|)^2 - 2\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$ and the right-hand side is always nonnegative. We begin by taking the derivative of the left-hand side and setting it equal to zero. The derivative is given by $$2(x_1 + x_2)x_1' + 2(y_1 + y_2)y_1' = 0,$$where we have used the fact that $f(x_1, y_1) = x_1 + x_2$ and $f(x_2, y_2) = y_1 + y_2.$

A quick sketch shows that this equation has a unique solution such that $x_1 = x_2 = \frac{1}{4}$ and $y_1 = y_2 = \frac{3}{8}. This is possible is equivalent to setting $|z_1 + z_2| = 1$, giving the maximal possible value of $|z_1 + z_2|$.

Substituting $z_1$ and $z_2$, we have $$|z_1 + z_2| = \boxed{\frac{\sqrt{17}}{4}}.$$
