The sum of the divisors of a number with prime factorization $p_1^{a_1}p_2^{a_2}\cdots p_n^{a_n}$ is given by:
\begin{align*}
\sigma(n) &= (1 + p_1 + p_1^2 + \cdots + p_1^{a_1})(1 + p_2 + p_2^2 + \cdots + p_2^{a_2}) \\
&\quad \vdots (1 + p_n + p_n^2 + \cdots + p_n^{a_n}) \\
&= (p_1^{a_1} + p_1^{a_1 - 1} + p_1^{a_1 - 2} + \cdots + 1)(p_2^{a_2} + p_2^{a_2 - 1} + p_2^{a_2 - 2} + \cdots + 1) \\
&\quad \vdots (p_n^{a_n} + p_n^{a_n - 1} + p_n^{a_n - 2} + \cdots + 1)
\end{align*}
For our number, $n = 400 = 2^7$. So, the sum of the divisors is:
\begin{align*}
\sigma(400) &= (2^7 + 2^6 + 2^5 + 2^4 + 2^3 + 2^2 + 2) \\
&= (128 + 64 + 32 + 16 + 8 + 4 + 2) \\
&= 255
\end{align*}
Next, we need to find the prime factorization of 255:
\begin{align*}
255 &= (3)(5)(17)
\end{align*}
The number $255$ has $3$ distinct prime factors, which are $3$, $5$, and $17$.
